                <head>14. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>On July 16, 1957, the Council on Foreign Economic Policy decided to hold regular
                    meetings on Soviet economic penetration activities to determine appropriate
                    action by the United States Government. At its meeting on October 17, the
                    Council again discussed the topic of Soviet economic penetration. It “considered
                    the problems presented by the readiness of the Soviet Bloc to purchase
                    commodities of underdeveloped countries and provide capital, equipment and
                    technical know-how to gain positions of major influence, regardless of the
                    economic profit of the venture.” (CFEP Project Study; Eisenhower Library, CFEP
                    Records, 1954–1961)</p>
            </div>
